Description: CVE-2026-53613 - libmount: pin the target to prevent TOCTOU in restricted mount(8)
 In restricted (non-root, setuid) mount(8) the /etc/fstab target is validated
 and later re-resolved by mount(2). A local user that owns a writable
 ancestor of the fstab mountpoint can swap a path component (for example with
 renameat2(RENAME_EXCHANGE)) between the check and the mount and redirect the
 mount to a root-owned location.
 .
 Add a pinned O_PATH target fd (fd_target) to the libmount context. It is
 opened via the new ul_open_no_symlinks() helper (openat2() with
 RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS, O_NOFOLLOW fallback) in
 mnt_context_prepare_target() BEFORE the target is canonicalized (so that a
 symlink in any component of the user-controlled path makes the mount fail
 instead of being resolved) and closed on context reset. For restricted
 mounts mount(2) is then called on /proc/self/fd/<fd_target> instead of the
 path. After a successful mount the fd is re-opened so that additional
 propagation/remount calls apply to the mounted filesystem.
 .
 The helper defines __NR_openat2 and struct open_how locally: glibc 2.31 does
 not provide SYS_openat2, so a SYS_openat2 based implementation is silently
 compiled out on bullseye. If openat2() is unavailable the path is walked
 component by component with O_NOFOLLOW.
 .
 Backport for util-linux 2.36.1: the upstream hook_mount.c/hook_mount_legacy.c
 and the new mount API do not exist here, so the pinning is done in
 libmount/src/context_mount.c (do_mount() and do_mount_additional()) by using
 /proc/self/fd/N as the mount(2) target.
Origin: backport, https://github.com/util-linux/util-linux/commit/0b010025a0e429bc80355c94db86a843395d49e2
Author: Karel Zak <kzak@redhat.com>
Last-Update: 2026-10-16

